hundreds of thousands of syrians have  died  millions have been displaced countless  others remain illegally detained  or often tortured missing disappeared  for 10 years the world has watched  serious spiral into destruction  and bloodshed wafer mustafa  is a long way from syria now but she's  still haunted by the nightmares of her  broken nation  water's father ali was a critic of the  syrian regime  he'd been arrested a couple of times in  his hometown and moved to damascus  to lower his profile but in 2013  ali was seized by armed men and nobody  has heard from him since then  family fled syria his daughter wafer now  in berlin  we haven't heard from my dad since then  we haven't been told why he was taken  for what reason what what is he charged  with where  is he i mean for years now  we've been trying just to get an answer  for  one single question is he alive  or not she protests against the assad  regime driven by the  endless stories of suffering in her home  country  i think that the main source of my hope  is my memory  my memory of my dad every time i feel  that um  that this is just pointless  i see someone inside syria protesting in  idlib for example  and after 10 years under the russian  uh bombing uh demanding freedom and  saying that  we do not give up our freedom i mean i  just feel like  do i even have the right to give up hope  we meet her again  outside the russian embassy where wafer  regularly protests against the country's  support for assad's regime  each of these photos is of someone who  was detained  or disappeared or simply killed here  a family group all of them have gone  these pictures are a testament to lives  ripped away  in syria and she still disappeared till  today i mean  her faith is still unknown unfortunately  the war in syria unleashed horrors on  all sides from the barbarity of isis to  the  gruesome cruelty of other groups  but the syrian government stands accused  of callous crimes  of chemical attacks that targeted  schools and hospitals  of torture and murder on an unthinkable  scale  amina is another whose life was ripped  apart  she was detained and held in appalling  conditions her three brothers were  tortured and killed  by those loyal to president assad  um  but the fury isn't just about the horror  pain and destruction  it's also about the sense that the world  has shrugged  and ignored a decade of disaster in  syria  dictatorship  at the united nations russia and china  vetoed a resolution to hold assad  accountable in the criminal court  they've defended him and his government  constantly  but there are signs that the world is  taking syria seriously  in germany a former syrian agent has  been jailed for his involvement in  torture another man remains on trial  and there may be a pathway to holding  president assad personally accountable  and it starts here in the netherlands  the dutch government accuses syria of  breaking the international convention  against torture and says all such abuses  must come to an end if it doesn't get  that guarantee and  few think it will then the path ends  with  charges being levied against president  assad and his ministers here at the  international court of justice now the  problem is  this process would take a long time  years the flip side is  it wouldn't be subject to veto by  diplomats from russia  or china the netherlands have now been  joined by canada in this legal action  the dutch foreign minister told me he's  determined to see justice done  even if it takes time and patience we  have  statements by survivors  we we have those terrible pictures i  think a lot of people  have seen we have the eyewitness  so there is ample evidence  to be used in a court of law but first i  want now  to have serious conversations with the  syrian government i'm afraid  that the wheels of justice often grind  slowly so it might take a couple of  years  but we we will persevere so is it really  worth it  according to one of the lawyers involved  there is a moral duty to act  he says the carnage of syria is a result  of turning a blind eye to atrocities in  previous decades  we are unfortunately paying the cost for  for not having  intervened over the last 40 years in  syria  by what we've seen with more than half a  million people being killed since 2011.  so i think if we let me put it this way  we cannot afford to take the risk  of not taking action now  back in berlin waffle works on she is  exhausted and traumatized but keeps  going  for the sake of her father she hasn't  seen for nearly a decade  who may not even be alive i'm not sure  till when i can do this but i'm sure  that this  is what i want to do and i am sure that  at least today i'm still doing this i  hope that tomorrow i will still be able  to do it  but i'm also sure that if one day  i collapsed and i wasn't able to  continue  i know that my dad will still be proud  that  one word syria has become synonymous  with  conflict brutality and suffering  a decade on the legacy of misery and  displacement  still rolls around the world adam  parsons sky news  berlin
 

